      <item>
         <title><![CDATA[Despite early start, Canada's flu season may be 'far from peak' as holidays approach]]></title>
         <link>https://www.cbc.ca/news/health/flu-season-november-h1n1-9.7427025?cmp=rss</link>
         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/1ee0891f-18c8-4353-9a86-c255d2e1250f,1795117365918/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%281%2C0%2C4238%2C2383%29%3B' alt='A pharmacist holds up a flu shot at Remedy&apos;s Rx Pharmacy in Ottawa on Nov. 19.' width='620' height='349' title='A pharmacist holds up a flu shot at Remedy&apos;s Rx Pharmacy in Ottawa on Nov. 19. Though influenza A is circulating right now, and could peak before the holiday season, medical experts say it&apos;s still worth getting a flu shot given the possibility of a second wave of infections later this winter.'/><p>Influenza appeared relatively early this fall, with a strain that often impacts younger adults. Medical experts aren't yet sure if this wave of infections will peter out before the holidays or if Canada will see a second surge of flu later in the winter.</p>]]></description>
         <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 13:22:37 EST</pubDate>
         <category>News/Health</category>
         <guid isPermaLink="false">9.7427025</guid>
         <dc:creator>Lauren Pelley</dc:creator>
